    <string name="meth_intro">How each metric in Statistics is calculated, with the exact formulas and the references they are based on.</string>
    <string name="meth_scale_title">Scale weight vs trend</string>
    <string name="meth_scale_body">«Current weight» is your latest entry. «Current trend» is the most recent value of the 7-day moving average. «Total change» compares the first and last entries of the selected range. «Minimum» and «maximum» are the extreme values of your entries within the range.</string>
    <string name="meth_ma_title">7-day moving average (trend)</string>
    <string name="meth_ma_window">W(d) = { entries dated in [d − 6, d] }</string>
    <string name="meth_ma_body">All your entries dated within the 7-day window ending on d are averaged. Days without entries are neither counted nor filled with zeros: if you only weighed yourself 3 times that week, the average is of those 3 entries. This smooths out daily noise (water, salt, gut content) while keeping the real fat signal, which changes slowly. It is the usual practice recommended in the self-weighing literature and the basis of the «trend weight» concept popularised by The Hacker\'s Diet.</string>
    <string name="meth_rate_title">Weekly rate (kg/week)</string>
    <string name="meth_rate_formula">rate = β × 7 [kg/week]</string>
    <string name="meth_rate_body">x is the number of days since the first entry in the range and y the recorded weight. The slope β is multiplied by 7 to express it per week. The regression uses your raw entries in the visible range: it captures the long-term direction better than comparing just two points.</string>
    <string name="meth_weekly_title">Weekly average weight</string>
    <string name="meth_weekly_body">Weeks start on Monday. The arithmetic mean of all entries in each week is used; weeks without entries are not shown.</string>
    <string name="meth_daily_title">Daily calories, protein, carbs and fat</string>
    <string name="meth_daily_body">Each day\'s total is the sum of that day\'s meals (calories, protein, carbs and fat are computed when saving, either by adding up the ingredients or taking your manual total). Days without meals count as 0, so gaps honestly reflect adherence instead of disappearing. The «average» is the arithmetic mean of the range, the «peak day» the maximum value, and the trend line uses exactly the same 7-day moving average described above.</string>
    <string name="meth_protein_title">Daily protein recommendations</string>
    <string name="meth_protein_body">How much protein you need depends on your goal, your weight and your body composition. The app calculates a daily range by multiplying your weight by a factor that depends on your goal.

Maintain muscle (1.2–1.6 g/kg/day): enough for most active people. Bulk (1.6–2.0 g/kg/day): above 1.6 g/kg the extra benefits start to diminish, but up to 2.0 covers individual variability. Cut (1.6–2.2 g/kg of body weight): during a calorie deficit protein helps preserve muscle.

The recommendation is based only on total body weight.</string>
    <string name="meth_tdee_title">Estimated daily energy expenditure (TDEE)</string>
    <string name="meth_tdee_men">Men: BMR = (10 × weight_kg) + (6.25 × height_cm) − (5 × age) + 5</string>
    <string name="meth_tdee_women">Women: BMR = (10 × weight_kg) + (6.25 × height_cm) − (5 × age) − 161</string>
    <string name="meth_tdee_total">TDEE = BMR × activity factor</string>
    <string name="meth_tdee_body">Total energy expenditure is estimated in two steps: first the basal metabolic rate (BMR) is calculated with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation (1990), considered the most accurate for non-athletes (Frankenfield et al. 2005, 82% accurate within ±10%). It is then multiplied by an activity factor based on gym frequency, session length and daily walking time.

Activity factors range from 1.2 (sedentary) to 1.9 (very active). Research shows that people tend to overestimate their activity level, so the thresholds are calibrated conservatively.

Cut: TDEE − 400 kcal (range: −500 to −300). Maintain: TDEE ± 100 kcal. Bulk: TDEE + 300 kcal (range: +200 to +400).</string>
